1. What is Box Model?

Ans. The Box Model is a concept that explains how every HTML element is treated as a rectangular box on a webpage. It consists of four main parts: content, padding, border, and margin .

 Content:  The actual text, image, or other material inside the element.
 Padding:  The space between the content and the border.
 Border:  The line surrounding the padding and content.
 Margin:  The space outside the border that separates the element from other elements.

For example:
div {
    width: 200px;
    padding: 20px;
    border: 2px solid black;
    margin: 10px;
}
The box model helps us control the size, spacing, and appearance of elements on a webpage.
 
2. What is media query? Explain with a sample code.

Ans. A media query is a feature used to make a website responsive . It allows us to apply different styles depending on the screen size or device.

For example:
body {
    background-color: white;
}

@media (max-width: 600px) {
    body {
        background-color: lightblue;
    }
}
 

Here, the background will be white on larger screens. When the screen width becomes 600px or less, the background changes to light blue. Media queries are commonly used to make websites work properly on mobiles, tablets, and desktops.

 
